response:
 The auditor is referring to **ISO 45001:2018, sub-clause 8.1.2: Eliminating hazards and reducing OH&S risks**.

This sub-clause explicitly requires the hierarchy of controls:

> "When determining controls, or considering changes to existing controls, the organization shall reduce the risks according to the following hierarchy:
> a) eliminate the hazard;
> b) substitute with less hazardous processes, operations, materials or equipment;
> c) use engineering controls and reorganization of work;
> d) use administrative controls, including training;
> e) use adequate personal protective equipment."

The key phrase is **"reduce the risks according to the following hierarchy"** — this is a mandatory sequence, not a menu of options. The standard requires you to work through the hierarchy in order, applying each level as far as is reasonably practicable before moving to the next.

Your auditor's point is valid: documented procedures and PPE alone don't demonstrate compliance if you haven't first considered whether the hazard could be eliminated, substituted, or engineered out. You need evidence of that **decision-making process** — showing you considered elimination first and why it wasn't reasonably practicable, before defaulting to administrative controls and PPE.

This is also reinforced in **6.1.2 (Assessment of OH&S risks and other risks to the OH&S management system)**, which requires the methodology for assessing risks to be defined and applied, including the hierarchy of controls.
